A||N||3||TheFiero64||Help! V6 engine numbers or year||||9||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||03-13-2000||03:23 PM||||I think my engine is an 88. I have had a really bad vibration since I installed it in my 88 coupe. It came out of an 86 SE but had a reconstructed title, and the guy said it's not the original engine. I know it has an 86 flywheel. I found this online.<P>&gt;1985   2.8   CBX, CBY<BR>&gt;1986   2.8   DAF, DAH, DBN<BR>&gt;1987   2.8   SCN, SCP<BR>&gt;1988   2.8   CFC, CFD<BR>&gt;Engine number location: front of block below right cylinder head<P>But I can't find this code on my block. I did find a # on the block below the distributor toward the front. it said "J8888" if the J stands for the same date code in the vin then it would be an 88. The plenum is the engraved style. Please help I hope I've not done any damage driving it if it is an 88 engine. Thanks<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||03-13-2000||03:50 PM||||If indeed it is an 88 engine with a 86 flywheel, that could account for the vibration. I was thinking the 88 models had a neutrally balanced flywheel, which none of the earlier models used.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||03-13-2000||11:45 PM||||Here's what the Chevrolet Power book(7th ed.) says:<BR>"V6/60 crankshafts manufactured through the 1986 model year are externally balanced and require a counterweighted flywheel or flexplate for proper engine balance. In 1987, a new crankshaft with an integral timing disc was introduced in 2.8 liter engines with aluminum cylinder heads. Crankshafts with timing discs are internally balanced, and do not require a counterweighted flywheel." Reading on, it implies that this engine was used in front-drive models only. Don't know if that's true or not.<BR>But, at least it agrees with Batman.. er.. Cabana-boy.. er..Batboy.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||03-14-2000||11:22 AM||||I believe the 88 was the only V-6 Fiero that used a neutrally balance flywheel. If you've got an 88 motor, and an 86 flywheel, then that is probably your vibration. Another way to narrow it down (somewhat) is to look at the intake plenum. In 87, they started engraving "Fiero" in the top, instead of using a sticker. <P>Ummm... Cabana-boy? Hmm. ||||reg||1
